   "text": "I read this as a PROTAC-type molecule with three parts joined in a line. The stereochemistry comes from assuming the standard configurations of the known fragments, not from reading each wedge in the image.\n\n- **E3 ligand (left):** a VHL ligand of the VH032 type. It has a 4-methylthiazol-5-yl aryl group, a benzylamide, a 4-hydroxyproline, and a tert-leucine capped with a 1-fluorocyclopropanecarbonyl group. The aryl ring also carries an OCH2C(=O) ether linkage.\n- **Linker (middle):** the OCH2C(=O) group is attached to a piperidine nitrogen. The piperidine C4 carries a three-carbon chain, CH2CH2CH2, ending in NH\u2013C(=O)\u2013O, which is a carbamate.\n- **Statin (right):** a lovastatin-type hexahydronaphthalene core with the same ring double bonds and two methyl groups. The carbamate oxygen sits where lovastatin has its 2-methylbutanoate ester. The core carries the CH2CH2 chain to the 4-hydroxy-6-oxo-tetrahydropyran lactone.\n\nThe stereocentres on the VHL proline and tert-leucine, and on the statin core and lactone, are taken from the VH032 and lovastatin SMILES. I wrote the statin part in PubChem's lovastatin form with the ester swapped for the carbamate, then spliced it into the full SMILES.\n\nSMILES: Cc1ncsc1-c1ccc(CNC(=O)[C@@H]2C[C@@H](O)CN2C(=O)[C@@H](NC(=O)C2(F)CC2)C(C)(C)C)c(OCC(=O)N2CCC(CCCNC(=O)O[C@H]3C[C@H](C=C4[C@H]3[C@H]([C@H](C=C4)C)CC[C@@H]5C[C@H](CC(=O)O5)O)C)CC2)c1"
  }
